{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0116k9KUWByKRavXX5k5AvzH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Go 1.25.0. Let me check if Go 1.25 has `os.IsTerminal`... Actually, Go doesn't have that in the standard library as of my knowledge. The standard way is `golang.org/x/term.IsTerminal()` or using the `syscall` package directly.\n\nSince D1 says standard library only, I'll use `syscall` for terminal detection. On Unix-like systems, we can use the `unix.IoctlGetWinsize` approach, but `syscall` is standard lib.\n\nActually, for Go 1.21+, there's `golang.org/x/term` but that's not stdlib. \n\nI'll implement terminal detection using the `syscall` package with an ioctl call to check if a file descriptor is connected to a terminal.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the platform-specific constants are a problem here—macOS uses `TIOCGETA` while Linux uses `TCGETS`. Rather than dealing with build tags and platform-specific code, I should check if Go's standard library already has a built-in solution like `os.IsTerminal` or `term.IsTerminal` that handles this for me.\n\nSince we're on Go 1.25, I'll implement a portable helper using the `syscall` package with the `TIOCGWINSZ` ioctl call...\n\nWriting terminal detection helper... I'm realizing `syscall.TIOCGWINSZ` is actually defined in Go's syscall package for both Darwin and Linux, so I can use it directly. Rather than getting bogged down in implementation details, I should focus on what the task actually requires—creating size.go and color.go files. For terminal detection in the color file, I'll use a replaceable function variable approach that's both clean and testable, with a default implementation that can be overridden during testing. I need to make this testable, so let me outline the full implementation. I'm setting up the format package with a HumanSize function that handles both binary and SI unit conversions, starting with the copyright header and defining 